{
"p": "The **weak genitive** also marks the person who **gains or loses** by the action, where English uses \"on me\", \"for me\" or a possessive:"
},
{
"ex": [
{
"el": "Μου έκλεψαν το πορτοφόλι!",
"en": "My wallet was stolen! (lit. they stole the wallet on me)"
},
{
"el": "Μου χάλασε το αυτοκίνητο.",
"en": "My car broke down (on me)."
},
{
"el": "Θα σου κρατήσω μια θέση.",
"en": "I'll keep a seat for you."
},
{
"el": "Μου κόπηκε το ρεύμα.",
"en": "My electricity got cut off."
},
{
"el": "Μη μου κλαις τώρα!",
"en": "Don't go crying on me now!"
}
]
},
{
"p": "The last one is the **ethic genitive**: it adds feeling, not information, and is very common in speech: **Μου μεγάλωσες!** \"Look how you've grown!\""
},
{
"tip": "Two weak pronouns: **genitive first, accusative second**: **μου το, σου τα, της τον**. **Δώσε μου το → Δώσ' μου το**, and before the verb **Θα σου το φέρω**."
},
{
"solved": [
{
"q": "\"They charged us twenty euros.\"",
"a": "Μας χρέωσαν είκοσι ευρώ.",
"why": "χρεώνω + accusative μας."
},
